Q: c++ futures/promises like javascript? A: <p>This question is quite old, and the C++ ecosystem has changed a lot since it was originally asked. If what you want is the exact JS pattern — <code>.then()</code>, <code>.catch()</code>, chaining, same‑thread continuation, <code>all</code>, <code>race</code>, etc. — you need a continuation‑based promise abstraction.</p>
<p>One option in modern C++23 is:</p>
<p>alx-home/JSProCpp <a href="https://github.com/alx-home/JSProCpp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/alx-home/JSProCpp</a></p>
<p>It provides a JavaScript‑like promise API built on top of coroutines:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;promise/promise.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;

WPromise GetAnswer {[] -&gt; Promise&lt;int&gt; { 
    co_return 42; 
}};

// Returning a promise handle (not a coroutine) uses WPromise.
WPromise FetchCachedOrAsync(bool use_cache) {
    if (use_cache) {
        return Promise&lt;int&gt;::Resolve(42);
    }
    return []() -&gt; Promise&lt;int&gt; { co_return 42; };
}


WPromise Demo {[]() -&gt; Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    auto result = co_await GetAnswer();

    auto chained = WPromise{[=]() -&gt; Promise&lt;int&gt; {
        co_return result + 1;
    }}
        .Then([](int value) { return value * 2; })
        .Then([](int value) -&gt; Promise&lt;int&gt; { co_return value / 2; })
        .Then([](int value) -&gt; WPromise&lt;int&gt; {
            return FetchCachedOrAsync(value &gt; 0);
        })
        .Catch([](std::exception_ptr) -&gt; WPromise&lt;void&gt; {
            return Promise&lt;void&gt;::Resolve();
        })
        .Then([](std::optional&lt;int&gt; const&amp; value) -&gt; Promise&lt;int&gt; {
            // value is std::optional&lt;int&gt; because Catch returned void
            co_return value.value_or(-1);
        });

    [[maybe_unused]] auto value = co_await chained;
    (void)value;
    co_return;
}};
</code></pre>
<p>So yes, this pattern is possible in modern C++ — you need a continuation‑capable promise type.</p>

Q: JaCoCo indicates missed branch on two-part RegEx matches/find which is proved to be covered between multiple unit tests A: <p>Apart from the non-idempotence of <code>find()</code> (see rzwitersloot's answer), that third condition can be simplified.</p>
<p>If the condition <code>matcher.matches() &amp;&amp; !uuidMatcher.find()</code> is reported as missing a branch it's most likely you forgot one of the possible outcomes.</p>
<ul>
<li>If <code>matcher.matches()</code> returns <code>true</code> and <code>uuidMatcher.find()</code> returns <code>false</code> the condition is met and the matching statement is invoked. This obviously occurs, since the statement is marked as green.</li>
<li>If <code>matcher.matches()</code> returns <code>true</code> and <code>uuidMatcher.find()</code> returns <code>true</code> the condition isn't met and the final <code>else</code> is invoked.</li>
<li>If <code>matcher.matches()</code> returns <code>false</code> the condition isn't met and the final <code>else</code> is invoked.</li>
</ul>
<p>The following shows that the second possible outcome will never actually check the result of <code>find()</code>, even if it were idempotent:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>boolean matches = matcher.matches();
boolean find = uuidMatcher.find();
if (matches &amp;&amp; find)
    System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (1): matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
else if (!matches &amp;&amp; find)
    System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (2): !matcher.matches &amp; uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
else if (matches &amp;&amp; !find)
    // At this point, matches &amp;&amp; find is false and !matches &amp;&amp; find is false.
    // That means that you've already tested both possible conditions with find being true,
    // and you can omit it.
    // After all, if matches is true then find is automatically false at this point,
    // otherwise the if's condition would have been met
    System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (3): matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
else
    System.out.println(&quot;Satisfied (4): !matcher.matches &amp; !uuidMatcher.find&quot;);
</code></pre>
